<description>
Crop out the separator cross and return the quadrant containing the single marker pixel.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background color and exactly two foreground colors.
- One foreground color forms a unique solid row and solid column of the same
  color, splitting the grid into four nonempty regions.
- The other foreground color appears as one marker pixel in one of those
  regions.

Rule
- Find the unique same-color solid row/column separator cross.
- Select the region on the same side of the separator row and column as the
  marker pixel.
- Return that region unchanged, which removes the separator cross and keeps
  the marker at its local position.

Key insight
- The cross is not part of the output pattern; it is a divider telling which
  quadrant-sized crop to keep.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "grid partitioned into four regions by a one-pixel solid separator cross, with one non-separator marker pixel in a region",
  "transform": "crop to the region containing the marker pixel, excluding the separator row and column; preserve the marker and background colors",
  "grid_shape": "one separator-free region of the cross-partitioned grid"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid:
    shape: (2N+1) x (2N+1) square, N in 2-8
    N observed range: 2-6
  colors:
    exactly 3 distinct colors -- background, separator, marker
    all three are different; any ARC color (0-9) allowed
  separator_cross:
    one solid row and one solid col of separator color
    always at center position (N, N), dividing grid into 4 equal NxN quadrants
  marker:
    exactly 1 pixel of marker color, placed in one of the 4 quadrants
    not on the separator row or column
  quadrants:
    the quadrant containing the marker has bg + 1 marker pixel
    the other 3 quadrants are pure background
  output:
    the NxN quadrant containing the marker, extracted as-is

generation_strategy:
  summary: direct construction (no inversion needed; task is simple cropping)
  steps:
    1. pick N from 2-8, set grid size to (2N+1) x (2N+1)
    2. pick 3 distinct ARC colors for background, separator, marker
    3. fill grid with background color
    4. draw separator cross at row N and col N
    5. choose one of 4 quadrants (TL/TR/BL/BR) uniformly at random
    6. place 1 marker pixel at a random position within that quadrant
    7. output = the chosen NxN quadrant with its marker pixel
```
</generation_spec>
